# Duck typing
# = making method calls on arguments rather than assuming/testing the class of
# arguments
# "If it walks like a duck and quacks like a duck, it doesn't necessarily mean
# it's a duck, but I don't care since it does the job"
# When writing a method I might want to test that it gets absolutely a Foo
# argument, but really I need an object with enough methods similar to Foo that
# I can call in the body of my method, not necessarily a Foo as long as it has
# the right methods
# It allows more code reuse = you only care about an object is what messages
#you can send it

#doesn't necessarily expect an object instance of class Point and use its
#getters/setters for instance variable x to negate it in place (create
#reflection mirror)

# Actually this method takes anything with method x= and x where result of x
# has a * method (not necessarily multiplication) that takes -1. Sends result
# of calling x the * message with -1 and sends that result to x=
def mirror_update pt
  pt.x = pt.x * (-1)
end
